"""Fuller entries for November and December saints (original text). See tools/SAINTS_BRIEF.md."""
EXTRA_NOVDEC = {
 "11-03": dict(
  life="Martin was born in Lima in 1579 to Juan de Porres, a Spanish nobleman, and Ana Velázquez, a freed black woman from Panama. His father was slow to acknowledge him, and the boy grew up poor. He was apprenticed to a barber-surgeon and learned to dress wounds and prepare remedies. At about fifteen he went to the Dominican priory of the Holy Rosary, where the law of the time allowed a man of his birth only to serve as a lay helper; in 1603 he was professed as a lay brother. He swept, shaved, nursed and begged alms, setting up care for orphans and the sick poor of the city. He was a friend of Saint Rose of Lima, and many stories tell of his healings and his gentleness with animals. He died in 1639 and was canonised by John XXIII in 1962.",
  prayerTo="Saint Martin de Porres, you were looked down on and answered with humble love, serving the sick, the poor and the forgotten of Lima. Pray for all who suffer because of their race or birth, and for me, that I may see Christ in each person I meet and serve Him gladly in the lowliest tasks. Amen.",
  penance="Do one humble, practical task today that others avoid, and give alms to a charity that cares for the sick poor."),
 "11-04": dict(
  life="Charles was born in 1538 at the castle of Arona on Lake Maggiore. When his uncle became Pope Pius IV in 1559, he made Charles a cardinal at twenty-one, administrator of the archdiocese of Milan and his chief assistant in Rome. Charles helped bring the Council of Trent to its close in 1563 and oversaw the Roman Catechism that followed it. The sudden death of his elder brother turned him to a more serious life; he was ordained priest and bishop and in 1566 went to live in Milan, which had not had a resident archbishop for decades. He held synods, founded seminaries, taught the catechism to children and visited remote Alpine parishes on foot. In 1569 a member of a religious order he was reforming shot at him while he prayed; he was unhurt. Worn out by work and penance, he died in 1584, aged forty-six.",
  prayerTo="Saint Charles, shepherd of Milan, you spent yourself for the reform of the Church and did not abandon your people in the plague. Pray for bishops and priests, that they may be faithful and fearless, and for me, that I may begin the renewal of the Church with the conversion of my own heart. Amen.",
  penance="Examine your conscience carefully tonight and make a plan to go to Confession this week. Visit or help someone who is sick, as Charles did in the plague."),
 "11-09": dict(
  life="The Lateran takes its name from the Laterani, a Roman family whose palace passed to the emperor Constantine. After he ended the persecution, Constantine gave the property to the Bishop of Rome, and a great basilica was built beside it and dedicated to Christ the Saviour; later the two Saints John, the Baptist and the Evangelist, were added to its title. For about a thousand years the popes lived at the Lateran, and five general councils of the Church met there, including the Fourth Lateran Council of 1215. The basilica has been ruined by earthquake and fire and rebuilt more than once; its present façade dates from the eighteenth century. The feast was extended to the whole Church as a sign of love and unity with the See of Peter.",
  prayerTo="Lord God, you build your Church from living stones, chosen and precious. As we honour the cathedral of the Bishop of Rome, unite us more closely with the Holy Father and with one another. Make my own heart a fit dwelling for your Spirit, and keep every parish church a house of prayer for all peoples. Through Christ our Lord. Amen.",
  penance="Pray for the Pope today, and make a visit to your parish church. Give something towards its upkeep or its work for the poor."),
 "11-10": dict(
  life="Leo was a deacon of the Roman Church, trusted with delicate missions, and was away in Gaul when he was elected pope in 440. In 449 he sent his Tome to Bishop Flavian of Constantinople, setting out the faith that the one Christ is truly God and truly man, without confusion and without division. When a council at Ephesus that year rejected it by violence, Leo called it a robbery; two years later the Council of Chalcedon received his teaching. In 452 he went out to meet Attila near Mantua and the Huns withdrew from Italy. In 455 he could not prevent the Vandals from sacking Rome, but he persuaded their king to spare the city from burning and slaughter. Nearly a hundred of his sermons survive, clear and dignified. He died in 461 and was named a Doctor of the Church in 1754.",
  prayerTo="Saint Leo, faithful successor of Peter, you taught the Church to confess Christ as true God and true man, and you stood before invaders for your people. Pray that I may hold the faith clearly and humbly, remember the dignity God has given me, and not lose courage when the world around me is shaken. Amen.",
  penance="Read slowly the Church's confession of Christ in the Creed, and pray it for those who have lost their faith. Leo urged fasting joined to almsgiving: give the price of a meal to the poor."),
 "11-11": dict(
  life="Martin was born around 316 at Sabaria in Pannonia, in present-day Hungary, the son of an army officer, and grew up at Pavia. Drawn to Christ as a boy, he was enrolled in the cavalry at fifteen. After he shared his cloak with the beggar at Amiens he was baptised, and some time later he left the army, telling the emperor that he was now a soldier of Christ. He became a disciple of Saint Hilary of Poitiers and founded at Ligugé the first monastery known in Gaul. In 371 the people of Tours chose him as their bishop against his will. He lived as a monk at Marmoutier outside the city, travelled the countryside preaching, and was known for his care of prisoners and the poor. He died at Candes in 397. His life was written by his friend Sulpicius Severus.",
  prayerTo="Saint Martin, soldier of Christ, you saw the Lord in a freezing beggar and gave him half of what you had. Pray for soldiers and for all who suffer the cost of war, and for me, that I may not pass by the poor but share with them freely, as Christ has shared Himself with me. Amen.",
  penance="Give a warm coat or blanket to a shelter or clothing bank today, and pray for those who have died in war."),
 "11-15": dict(
  life="Albert was born around 1200 at Lauingen in Swabia and studied at Padua, where he joined the young Order of Preachers. He taught in Dominican houses across Germany and then at Paris and Cologne. Among his students was a large, silent Italian whom the others called the dumb ox; Albert is said to have replied that the ox's bellowing would one day be heard throughout the world. The student was Thomas Aquinas. Albert wrote on almost every subject: Scripture and theology, the works of Aristotle, plants, animals, minerals and the stars, and he valued careful observation. In 1260 he was made bishop of Regensburg and resigned after two years to return to teaching. In old age he defended the teaching of Thomas, who had died before him. He died at Cologne in 1280 and was canonised and named a Doctor of the Church in 1931.",
  prayerTo="Saint Albert, you studied the works of God with patience and wonder and saw no quarrel between faith and reason. Pray for scientists, teachers and students, that they may seek the truth honestly and humbly. Help me to use my mind in God's service and to give Him thanks for all that I learn. Amen.",
  penance="Spend half an hour today learning something about the natural world, and end it with a prayer of thanks to the Creator. Give up an hour of idle scrolling to do it."),
 "11-16": dict(
  life="Margaret was born around 1045 in Hungary, where her father, an English prince, lived in exile; she was a granddaughter of King Edmund Ironside. The family came to England in 1057, and after the Norman Conquest they fled north. About 1070 she married Malcolm III of Scotland. Of their eight children, three sons became kings of Scots and a daughter became queen of England. Her confessor Turgot, who wrote her life, tells that she rose at night to pray, read the Scriptures, and washed the feet of the poor and fed orphans before taking her own meal. She brought Benedictine monks from Canterbury to Dunfermline and encouraged councils that brought Scottish customs for Lent and Easter into line with the wider Church. Her small Gospel book, once dropped in a river and found undamaged, survives in Oxford. She died in 1093, days after her husband and eldest son were killed in battle.",
  prayerTo="Saint Margaret, wife, mother and queen, you made your household a place of prayer and your table a place of welcome for the poor. Pray for husbands and wives, for parents and children, and for the peoples of Scotland and England. Help me to serve those I live with, patiently and gladly. Amen.",
  penance="Before your own meal today, prepare or give food for someone in need, perhaps through a food bank. Read a chapter of the Gospels, as Margaret did daily."),
 "11-17": dict(
  life="Elizabeth was born in 1207, a daughter of King Andrew II of Hungary, and at four was sent to the Wartburg castle to be raised with Louis of Thuringia, her future husband. They married when she was fourteen and loved each other deeply; they had three children. Influenced by the Franciscan friars newly come to Germany, she gave generously to the poor, and in a famine she opened the granaries while Louis was away. In 1227 Louis died of fever on his way to the Crusade. Elizabeth left the court, made provision for her children and took the habit of the Franciscan Third Order. With her dowry she built a hospital at Marburg in honour of Saint Francis and nursed the sick there with her own hands, under the strict direction of Conrad of Marburg. She died in 1231 and was canonised four years later.",
  prayerTo="Saint Elizabeth, you were a princess who chose to be a servant, and you found Christ in the sick and the hungry. Pray for all who care for the suffering, for widows and the bereaved, and for me, that I may hold loosely to what I own and give freely to those in need. Amen.",
  penance="Give away something you value but do not need, or volunteer an hour at a hospital, care home or soup kitchen."),
 "11-21": dict(
  life="The story of Mary's presentation comes from the Protoevangelium of James, a second-century writing not part of Scripture. It tells that Joachim and Anne, who had long been childless, brought their daughter at the age of three to the Temple, where she was received by the priest and lived in prayer until her betrothal to Joseph. The Church does not vouch for these details, but honours the truth they point to: that Mary belonged wholly to God from the beginning. The feast began in Jerusalem with the dedication of the New Church of Saint Mary in 543. It is one of the great feasts of the Eastern Churches, and spread in the West from the fourteenth century. Since 1953 the day has also been kept as a day of prayer for enclosed contemplative nuns.",
  prayerTo="Holy Mary, from your earliest years you were given wholly to God and kept nothing back. Pray for me, that I may offer myself to Him without reserve, as I am today. Pray for all who live hidden lives of prayer in the cloister, and keep us faithful to the Lord who has called us. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for enclosed nuns and monks, and if you can, send a gift or a note of thanks to a contemplative community."),
 "11-22": dict(
  life="Cecilia was venerated in Rome from early times, and her name stands in the Roman Canon of the Mass. The story of her life comes from her Acts, written long after her death and largely legendary. They tell that she was a Christian of noble family who had vowed her virginity to God and was married against her will to Valerian, a pagan. On their wedding night she told him of her vow and of the angel who guarded her; Valerian was baptised, and with his brother Tiburtius was martyred. Cecilia was then condemned, survived an attempt to suffocate her in the bath of her house, and died of wounds from the sword. The church of Santa Cecilia in Trastevere is said to stand on the site of her home. She became the patron of musicians because an antiphon speaks of music played at her wedding.",
  prayerTo="Saint Cecilia, virgin and martyr, you sang to the Lord in your heart while the music of the world played around you. Pray for musicians and all who lead the Church's song, and for me, that my whole life may become a hymn of praise, faithful to Christ in every trial. Amen.",
  penance="Sing a hymn today, alone or with others, or support your parish choir. Keep a quiet hour without background music, to listen to God."),
 "11-25": dict(
  life="Catherine has been loved since at least the eighth century, but almost nothing about her can be shown from history, and her story belongs to tradition. It tells that she was a young woman of noble birth and great learning in Alexandria who was converted to Christ. When the emperor persecuted the Christians she confronted him, and he summoned fifty philosophers to refute her; she won them to the faith, and they were put to death. Refusing his offer of marriage, she was condemned to a wheel set with spikes, which broke apart, and was beheaded. Her body, it is said, was carried by angels to Mount Sinai, where the ancient monastery bears her name. She was counted among the Fourteen Holy Helpers. Removed from the general calendar in 1969 for lack of historical evidence, her memorial was restored in 2002.",
  prayerTo="Saint Catherine, tradition remembers you as wise and fearless, a young woman who defended the faith before the powerful. Pray for students, teachers and philosophers, that they may seek and love the truth. Help me to understand my faith well and to speak of it with gentleness and courage when I am questioned. Amen.",
  penance="Study a short section of the Catechism today. Give up some time you would spend on entertainment to do it."),
 "11-30": dict(
  life="Andrew was a fisherman from Bethsaida who worked with his brother Simon on the Sea of Galilee. Saint John's Gospel tells that he was a disciple of John the Baptist, and that when the Baptist pointed to Jesus as the Lamb of God, Andrew followed Him and asked where He was staying. 'Come and see,' Jesus answered, and Andrew spent the day with Him. He went at once to tell Simon, 'We have found the Messiah.' Later it was Andrew who found the boy with five barley loaves and two fish, and who, with Philip, brought some Greeks to Jesus. Tradition holds that he preached around the Black Sea and in Greece and was crucified at Patras. He is the patron of Scotland, of Greece and of the Church of Constantinople, and his feast is a day of friendship between Rome and the Orthodox.",
  prayerTo="Saint Andrew, you left your nets when Christ called and went at once to bring your brother to Him. Pray for me, that I may follow the Lord without delay and share Him with those I love. Pray for the unity of Christians of East and West, and for the people of Scotland. Amen.",
  penance="Invite someone to Mass, or speak a word about your faith to one person who does not know Christ. Pray for the unity of Christians."),
 "12-03": dict(
  life="Francis was born in 1506 at the castle of Xavier in Navarre. At the University of Paris he shared rooms with Peter Faber and then with Ignatius of Loyola, who slowly won the ambitious young teacher for Christ. In 1534 the friends made their vows at Montmartre, the beginning of the Society of Jesus. In 1541, at the request of the King of Portugal, Francis sailed for the Indies and reached Goa the next year. He taught the faith to the fishing people of southern India, then worked in Malacca and the Moluccas, ringing a bell in the streets to call children to the catechism. In 1549 he reached Japan and spent two years there, adapting his ways to its culture. Hoping to enter China, he fell ill on the island of Shangchuan and died there in 1552. His body lies at Goa.",
  prayerTo="Saint Francis Xavier, you gave up your ambitions to carry the Gospel to the ends of the earth. Pray for missionaries, and for all who have not yet heard of Christ. Stir up in me a zeal for souls, and help me to bring the love of God to the people around me, wherever He sends me. Amen.",
  penance="Give an offering to a missionary society today, and pray a decade of the Rosary for missionaries and the peoples they serve."),
 "12-06": dict(
  life="Nicholas was bishop of Myra in Lycia, on the south coast of present-day Turkey, in the fourth century. Few facts about him are certain, but his cult was strong in East and West from early times. Tradition says he was born at Patara to wealthy Christian parents, gave away his inheritance, suffered imprisonment in the persecution under Diocletian and attended the Council of Nicaea. Other stories tell how he saved three innocent men from execution and calmed a storm for sailors. In 1087 sailors from Bari carried his relics from Myra to their own city, where they rest in the Basilica of San Nicola. He is the patron of children, sailors and Russia. The Dutch kept his feast with gifts for children as Sinterklaas, and from that custom came Santa Claus.",
  prayerTo="Saint Nicholas, generous shepherd, you gave in secret to save the poor from shame and despair. Pray for children, for families in need, and for all who travel by sea. Teach me to give without being seen, and to prepare for Christmas with a heart turned towards others. Amen.",
  penance="Give a gift anonymously today to someone in need, and give a child a small present or treat in honour of Saint Nicholas."),
 "12-07": dict(
  life="Ambrose was born around 340 at Trier, the son of a high Roman official, and trained in law. As governor of the province around Milan he went to the cathedral in 374 to keep order at a bitter election for bishop. Tradition says a child's voice cried 'Ambrose for bishop', and the crowd took it up. He was still a catechumen; within a week he was baptised and ordained, and he was consecrated on 7 December. He gave his wealth to the poor and studied the Scriptures and the Greek Fathers. When the empress Justina demanded a church for the Arians, he occupied it with his people, teaching them hymns to sing through the night. He refused Communion to the emperor Theodosius until he did penance for a massacre at Thessalonica. At the Easter Vigil of 387 he baptised Augustine. He died on Holy Saturday, 397.",
  prayerTo="Saint Ambrose, faithful bishop, you preached the word with power, sang the praises of God and stood firm before emperors. Pray for bishops and for all who teach the faith, and for me, that I may love the Scriptures, speak the truth with courage, and help others find their way to Christ. Amen.",
  penance="Pray part of the Divine Office today, Lauds or Vespers, which still uses Ambrose's hymns. If you have wronged someone in a way others saw, make it right openly."),
 "12-08": dict(
  life="The feast of Mary's conception was kept in the East from about the eighth century, and in England it was celebrated at Winchester and Canterbury before the Norman Conquest. Theologians long debated how Mary could be free of original sin when all are redeemed by Christ. The Franciscan John Duns Scotus answered that she was redeemed in a more perfect way, preserved from sin rather than freed from it, in view of her Son's merits. Pope Sixtus IV approved the feast in 1476, and Clement XI extended it to the whole Church in 1708. On 8 December 1854 Pius IX defined the doctrine as revealed by God, in the bull Ineffabilis Deus. At Lourdes in 1858 the Lady told Bernadette her name, words the Church has approved as private revelation. In many countries today is a holy day of obligation.",
  prayerTo="Immaculate Virgin Mary, all fair and full of grace, God preserved you from every stain of sin to be the worthy Mother of His Son. Look kindly on me, a sinner. Help me to hate sin and love purity of heart, and lead me by your prayers to the Saviour who redeemed you first and redeems us all. Amen.",
  penance="Go to Mass and, if you can, to Confession this Advent. Pray 'O Mary, conceived without sin, pray for us who have recourse to thee' each time you are tempted today."),
 "12-12": dict(
  life="Juan Diego was a Nahua man, born around 1474, who had been baptised by Franciscan missionaries a few years after the Spanish conquest of Mexico. On 9 December 1531, walking to Mass, he heard music on the hill of Tepeyac and saw a young woman who spoke to him in his own language and asked for a church to be built there. Bishop Juan de Zumárraga asked for a sign. On 12 December, hurrying to find a priest for his dying uncle, Juan Diego tried to avoid the hill, but the Lady met him, promised his uncle's healing and sent him to gather roses on its frozen summit. When he opened his cloak before the bishop, the roses fell and the image was there. The account was written down in Nahuatl, and the Church has approved the apparitions as private revelation. Juan Diego was canonised in 2002.",
  prayerTo="Our Lady of Guadalupe, you came to a poor man and spoke to him tenderly in his own tongue. You are here, our mother. Take under your mantle the peoples of the Americas, the poor and the forgotten, mothers and their unborn children, and me in my troubles. Bring us all to your Son. Amen.",
  penance="Bring flowers to an image of Our Lady today, and give to a charity that supports mothers and babies in need."),
 "12-13": dict(
  life="Lucy was martyred at Syracuse in Sicily in the persecution under Diocletian, around 304, and an inscription from about 400 shows that her feast was already kept there. The details of her story come from later Acts. They tell that she was a young Christian of wealthy family who had resolved to give her life to God. Her mother, Eutychia, was ill, and the two went on pilgrimage to the tomb of Saint Agatha at Catania, where her mother was healed. Lucy then persuaded her to give the dowry to the poor. The man she had been promised to denounced her as a Christian. The governor ordered her taken to a place of shame, but tradition says she could not be moved, and she was killed by the sword. Later tradition linked her with the eyes, and she is the patron of the blind.",
  prayerTo="Saint Lucy, your name means light, and you gave away all you had for Christ. Pray for the blind and for all who live in darkness of body or soul. Keep the eyes of my heart clear, that I may see what God asks of me, and help me to be a small light for others in these dark days. Amen.",
  penance="Light a candle in prayer today for someone going through a dark time, and give to a charity for the blind or partially sighted."),
 "12-14": dict(
  life="Juan de Yepes was born in 1542 at Fontiveros near Ávila. His father died young, and his mother brought up her sons in hard poverty. At Medina del Campo he worked in a hospital while studying with the Jesuits, and in 1563 he joined the Carmelites. Ordained in 1567, he was thinking of becoming a Carthusian when he met Teresa of Ávila, who persuaded him to help her reform. The next year at Duruelo he began the first house of the Discalced friars and took the name John of the Cross. In December 1577 friars opposed to the reform seized him and held him in their priory at Toledo, beaten and in darkness. There he composed much of the Spiritual Canticle. After nine months he escaped by night. In his last illness he was treated harshly by his own superior. He died at Úbeda in 1591 and was named a Doctor of the Church in 1926.",
  prayerTo="Saint John of the Cross, in a dark cell you found the living flame of God's love. Pray for all who walk through dryness, doubt or suffering, that they may trust that God is near. Teach me to let go of what holds me back from Him, and to seek Him above everything He gives. Amen.",
  penance="Give up one comfort today, offered freely for love of God. Spend ten minutes in silent prayer before a crucifix."),
 "12-26": dict(
  life="The Acts of the Apostles tells that when the Greek-speaking widows of Jerusalem were neglected in the daily distribution, the apostles chose seven men full of faith and the Holy Spirit to serve them, and Stephen was named first. He also worked wonders and preached with a wisdom his opponents could not answer. Accused falsely of speaking against the Temple and the Law, he was brought before the council, and his face seemed like the face of an angel. He answered with a long account of God's dealings with Israel, then cried out that he saw the heavens opened and the Son of Man standing at the right hand of God. He was dragged out and stoned. 'Lord Jesus, receive my spirit,' he prayed, and fell asleep. The persecution that followed scattered the Church and carried the Gospel beyond Jerusalem. His relics were found in 415.",
  prayerTo="Saint Stephen, first martyr, you served the poor and bore witness to Christ, and you died praying for those who stoned you. Pray for deacons and for all who suffer for the faith. Give me a share of your courage and your forgiving heart, that I may love even those who wrong me. Amen.",
  penance="Give alms today, in the old custom of Saint Stephen's Day, to someone who serves you during the year or to the poor. Pray by name for someone who has hurt you."),
 "12-27": dict(
  life="John was a son of Zebedee and, with his brother James, a fisherman on the Sea of Galilee. Jesus called the brothers the sons of thunder, and once they wanted to call down fire on a Samaritan village that would not receive Him. With Peter and James, John saw the raising of Jairus's daughter, the Transfiguration and the agony in Gethsemane. Tradition identifies him with the disciple whom Jesus loved, who ran with Peter to the empty tomb and recognised the risen Lord on the shore. After Pentecost he preached with Peter in Jerusalem, and Paul names him one of the pillars of the Church. Tradition holds that he was exiled to Patmos and lived to great age at Ephesus, where he cared for Mary. On his feast wine is blessed, recalling a legend that he drank a poisoned cup unharmed.",
  prayerTo="Saint John, beloved disciple, you rested on the heart of Christ and stood by His cross with His mother. Pray that I may know how much I am loved by God, and learn to love in return. Take me with you into the mystery of the Word made flesh, and teach me to love my neighbour. Amen.",
  penance="Read the first chapter of John's Gospel slowly today. Make a deliberate act of kindness to someone in your family."),
 "12-28": dict(
  life="Saint Matthew's Gospel tells that when the Magi did not return, King Herod, furious, ordered the killing of all the boys in Bethlehem and its district who were two years old or under, and that Joseph had already taken the Child and His mother to Egypt. Matthew sees in this the fulfilment of Jeremiah's words about Rachel weeping for her children. The deed fits what is known of Herod, who killed a wife and several of his own sons. Bethlehem was a small town, and the number of children was probably small, but each was a child known and loved. The Church has kept their feast since the fifth century, and they are honoured as martyrs who died not only for Christ but in His place. In England the day was once called Childermas.",
  prayerTo="Holy Innocents, little ones of Bethlehem, you gave your lives before you could speak His name. Pray for children who suffer violence, neglect or hunger, for children who have died, and for their grieving parents. Pray for all who hold power over the weak, that they may protect the smallest and most defenceless. Amen.",
  penance="Give to a charity that protects children, and pray today for parents who have lost a child."),
 "12-29": dict(
  life="Thomas was born in Cheapside, London, around 1120, to Norman parents. He entered the household of Theobald, Archbishop of Canterbury, who saw his talent, and in 1155 Henry II made him chancellor. Thomas served the king brilliantly, lived lavishly and became his close friend. In 1162 Henry had him made archbishop, expecting an ally. Instead Thomas resigned the chancellorship, took up a life of prayer and penance, and resisted the king's claim to try clergy in royal courts and control the Church's dealings with Rome. In 1164 he fled to France and spent six years in exile, for a time at the Cistercian abbey of Pontigny. He returned in December 1170. On 29 December four knights, taking angry words of the king as a command, killed him in his cathedral. He was canonised in 1173, and Henry did public penance at his tomb.",
  prayerTo="Saint Thomas of Canterbury, you turned from the favour of a king to serve the King of kings, and you died for the freedom of the Church. Pray for England and for all who hold authority, that they may govern justly. Give me courage to put God first, even at the cost of friendship or comfort. Amen.",
  penance="Pray for the Church in England and for those in public life. Give up something today that you hold onto for status or comfort."),
}
